        'lead' => 'STQC (Standardisation Testing and Quality Certification) is MeitY’s directorate for testing and certifying IT and electronics products, including CCTV cameras, and for security audits of government websites and applications.',
        'sections' => [
            ['STQC for CCTV cameras: Essential Requirements', '<p>CCTV cameras sold in India must meet MeitY’s <strong>Essential Requirements for Security of CCTV (ER-01)</strong> in addition to the safety standard IS 13252 (Part 1). They were added to the BIS Compulsory Registration Scheme in April 2024, and since <strong>9 April 2025</strong> BIS does not register CCTV models without an ER-01 security test report.</p><p>ER-01 testing covers hardware security (secure boot, debug port protection, secure storage), software security (secure code review, software bill of materials, signed and protected firmware updates), communication security, and supply-chain controls. For proprietary network protocols, the lab reviews the source code.</p><p>Since February 2026, the STQC security test report issued under the CRS order is also accepted for the Public Procurement (Make in India) order, so one test now serves both. See <a href="/certification/india/bis-crs-mark-certification">BIS CRS registration</a>.</p>'],
            ['STQC testing services', '<p>STQC runs labs across India for electronics and IT testing: electrical safety, EMC, environmental and reliability testing, calibration, and IT and software testing. Its schemes include certification of biometric devices used with Aadhaar, IoT device security under the IoT System Certification Scheme, and quality certification of IT products and services. Government buyers often ask for STQC reports in tenders.</p>'],
            ['STQC security audit for government websites and apps', '<p>Government departments usually need a security audit before a website or application goes live, and again after major changes. STQC and other auditors empanelled for government work test the application for vulnerabilities, including the OWASP Top 10, review the hosting and configuration, and issue a “safe to host” certificate once the findings are fixed. STQC also assesses websites against the Guidelines for Indian Government Websites (GIGW).</p>'],
