<li>FSSAI Central licence</li>
<li>Bank certificate or cancelled cheque</li>
<li>For manufacturer exporters: proof of the processing facility and its address</li>
<li>Details of the spices to be exported</li>
</ul>'],
            ['Online CRES registration steps', '<ol>
<li>Log in to the DGFT portal with your IEC.</li>
<li>Apply for RCMC/CRES and select the Spices Board as the issuing authority.</li>
<li>Choose manufacturer or merchant exporter, list the spices, and upload the documents.</li>
<li>Pay the fee online.</li>
<li>The Spices Board reviews the application and may inspect a manufacturer’s premises; the certificate is issued electronically.</li>
</ol><p>We can file this for you; see <a href="/services/indian-registration-compliances/business-licenses/spice-board-registration">Spice Board registration</a> and our <a href="/resources/blogs/spices-board-registration-cres-fees-process">CRES fees and process guide</a>.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['Do I need APEDA registration to export spices?', 'No. Spices listed under the Spices Board Act are registered with the Spices Board through CRES, not with APEDA. Products outside that list may fall under APEDA or another council.'],
            ['Where do I apply for CRES?', 'Apply online on the DGFT portal, selecting the Spices Board as the issuing authority. You need your IEC, PAN, GST and FSSAI Central licence.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'post',
        'key' => 'ce-vs-ukca-vs-ul-breakdown-global-product-safety-marks',
        'title' => 'UKCA vs CE vs UL: Key Differences Explained (2026)',
        'description' => 'UKCA vs CE vs UL marks compared: where each applies, who issues them, self-declaration vs third-party testing, and which one your product needs.',
        'answer' => '<p><strong>CE is the legal mark for the EU, UKCA for Great Britain, and UL is a private safety certification widely expected in the USA.</strong></p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>CE</th><th>UKCA</th><th>UL</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Market</td><td>EU and EEA (and Northern Ireland)</td><td>Great Britain</td><td>USA (UL’s Canadian mark for Canada)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legal status</td><td>Mandatory for products under EU product laws</td><td>Mandatory under UK law, but CE is still accepted for most products</td><td>Not legally required by federal law, but required by many buyers, retailers and workplace rules</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who applies it</td><td>Manufacturer, after self-declaration or a notified body</td><td>Manufacturer, after self-declaration or a UK approved body</td><td>UL or another recognised lab, after testing and factory follow-up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Factory inspections</td><td>Only for some routes</td><td>Only for some routes</td><td>Yes, regular follow-up visits</td></tr>
</tbody></table>',
        'sections' => [
            ['Is CE still accepted in Great Britain?', '<p>Yes. The UK government has decided to keep recognising CE marking indefinitely for most products covered by the regulations of the Department for Business and Trade, including electrical equipment, machinery, toys and radio equipment. Some sectors, such as medical devices, construction products and marine equipment, have their own rules and timelines. Products sold in Great Britain still need a UK importer address on the product or its documents. For UK-only products, see <a href="/certification/emea/ukca-certification">UKCA certification cost</a> and <a href="/certification/emea/ukca-certification">how to get UKCA marking</a>.</p>'],
            ['Where UL fits', '<p>UL is a private testing and certification company. In the USA, there is no federal law requiring a UL mark on consumer products, but workplace equipment must be certified by a Nationally Recognized Testing Laboratory (NRTL) under OSHA rules, electrical inspectors expect listed equipment under the National Electrical Code, and most large retailers ask for it. UL is the best-known NRTL; others, such as Intertek (ETL) and CSA, certify to the same UL standards. Radio and electronic devices also need <a href="/certification/americas/fcc-certification">FCC authorisation</a>, which is separate from UL.</p>'],
            ['Which mark does your product need?', '<table><thead><tr><th>Where you sell</th><th>Mark you need</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>EU countries</td><td>CE</td></tr>
<tr><td>Northern Ireland</td><td>CE (with UKNI where a UK body did the assessment)</td></tr>
<tr><td>England, Scotland and Wales</td><td>CE or UKCA, for most products</td></tr>
<tr><td>USA</td><td>FCC for electronics; UL or another NRTL listing for electrical safety when buyers, retailers or regulations ask for it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Several of these markets</td><td>Test once to IEC standards, for example with a <a href="/certification/global-certifications/cb-iecee">CB report</a>, then apply for each mark</td></tr>
</tbody></table>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is the difference between UKCA and CE?', 'CE is the EU’s conformity mark and UKCA is Great Britain’s. The requirements are similar, but UKCA relies on UK law, UK designated standards and UK approved bodies, while CE relies on EU law and EU notified bodies.'],
            ['Is CE accepted in the UK?', 'Yes, for most products. The UK has decided to recognise CE marking indefinitely for the main product regulations, so a CE-marked product can be sold in Great Britain, with some sector exceptions such as medical devices and construction products.'],
            ['Is UL mandatory in the USA?', 'Not under federal law for most consumer products. In practice, it is often required: workplaces must use equipment certified by a recognised testing lab, and most retailers and electrical inspectors expect it.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'certification/asia/mic-certificate',
        'title' => 'MIC Certification Japan (TELEC): Process & Cost',
        'description' => 'MIC / TELEC certification for radio products sold in Japan: technical standards, test reports, certification body, Giteki mark, timeline and cost.',
        'lead' => 'MIC certification (also called TELEC or Giteki) is Japan’s mandatory approval for radio equipment. Wireless products cannot be sold or used in Japan without it.',
        'sections' => [
            ['MIC, TELEC, Giteki and JATE: the names explained', '<ul>
<li><strong>MIC</strong> is Japan’s Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications, which regulates radio equipment under the Radio Law.</li>
<li><strong>TELEC</strong> is one of the Registered Certification Bodies that test and certify radio equipment, so “TELEC certification” is a common name for the same approval.</li>
<li><strong>Giteki</strong> is the mark that shows the product has the technical conformity certification.</li>
<li><strong>JATE</strong> approval is a different certification, under the Telecommunications Business Law, for terminal equipment that connects to public networks.</li>
</ul>'],
            ['The Giteki mark', '<p>Certified radio equipment carries the Giteki mark, a stylised R in a circle, with the certification number beside it. Terminal equipment also shows a T number. The mark goes on the product, or, for products with a screen, can be shown electronically in the settings menu. Each certified radio module carries its own number, and the host product can use that certification if it is installed as approved.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is MIC certification?', 'MIC certification is Japan’s mandatory technical conformity certification for radio equipment under the Radio Law. It is issued by a Registered Certification Body and shown with the Giteki mark.'],
            ['Is TELEC the same as MIC?', 'Effectively, yes. TELEC is one of the Registered Certification Bodies that issue certification under MIC’s rules, so “TELEC”, “MIC” and “Giteki” certification usually mean the same approval.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'services/indian-registration-compliances/import-export/fieo-membership',
        'title' => 'FIEO Membership 2026: Fees, Benefits & RCMC Process',
        'description' => 'FIEO membership for exporters: full form, membership fees by category, benefits, documents and how to get your RCMC through FIEO online.',
        'lead' => 'FIEO full form is Federation of Indian Export Organisations, the apex export promotion body that issues RCMC to multi-product exporters, service exporters, export-oriented units and SEZ units.',
        'sections' => [
            ['FIEO full form', '<p>FIEO stands for <strong>Federation of Indian Export Organisations</strong>, set up in 1965 by the Ministry of Commerce and private trade bodies. In Hindi: <span lang="hi">फियो का फुल फॉर्म है भारतीय निर्यात संगठनों का महासंघ (Federation of Indian Export Organisations)</span>.</p>'],
            ['Benefits of FIEO membership', '<ul>
<li><strong>RCMC:</strong> the registration-cum-membership certificate needed to claim benefits under the Foreign Trade Policy.</li>
<li><strong>Trade fairs and buyer-seller meets:</strong> subsidised participation in Indian pavilions abroad and reverse buyer-seller meets.</li>
<li><strong>Trade information:</strong> trade leads, market reports and help with country-specific requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Advocacy:</strong> FIEO represents exporters’ issues to the government.</li>
<li><strong>Certificates of origin:</strong> FIEO issues non-preferential certificates of origin to its members.</li>
</ul>'],
            ['FIEO vs other export promotion councils', '<p>Product-specific councils, such as EEPC for engineering goods, Pharmexcil for pharmaceuticals or the Spices Board for spices, issue RCMC to exporters of their products. FIEO issues it to exporters who deal in several products, to service exporters, to EOU and SEZ units, and to exporters whose products no specific council covers. If your products fall under a specific council, the Foreign Trade Policy expects you to register with that council. See <a href="/services/indian-registration-compliances/import-export/rcmc-certificate-registration">RCMC registration</a>.</p>'],
            ['FIEO membership fees', '<table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Turnover below ₹5 crore</th><th>Turnover ₹5 crore and above</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Multi-product exporter</td><td>₹7,000</td><td>₹7,850</td></tr>
<tr><td>Service provider</td><td>₹7,250</td><td>₹8,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>100% EOU</td><td>₹10,250</td><td>₹11,350</td></tr>
<tr><td>SEZ unit</td><td>₹10,750</td><td>₹11,500</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>These are FIEO’s published annual fees, excluding 18% GST. A one-time processing fee applies to a new e-RCMC, and FIEO gives a discount for paying several years in advance. Check the current schedule on FIEO’s website before paying.</p>'],
            ['Documents and online steps for FIEO RCMC', '<ol>
<li>Keep ready your IEC, PAN, GST registration, a bank certificate and the export turnover figures (with a chartered accountant’s certificate if asked).</li>
<li>Log in to the DGFT portal with your IEC and apply for RCMC, selecting FIEO as the issuing authority.</li>
<li>Choose your category and products, and upload the documents.</li>
<li>Pay the membership and processing fees online.</li>
<li>FIEO reviews the application and issues the e-RCMC, which you can download from the DGFT portal.</li>
</ol><p>You will need an <a href="/services/indian-registration-compliances/import-export/import-export-code-iec">IEC</a> before applying.</p>'],
            ['RCMC validity and renewal', '<p>An e-RCMC issued through FIEO is valid for five financial years, ending on 31 March of the fifth year, provided the membership fee is paid each year. Renew on the DGFT portal before it expires.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is FIEO?', 'FIEO, the Federation of Indian Export Organisations, is India’s apex export promotion body under the Ministry of Commerce. It issues RCMC to multi-product exporters, service exporters, EOUs and SEZ units, and supports exporters with trade fairs and information.'],
            ['What is the FIEO membership fee?', 'The annual fee for a multi-product exporter is ₹7,000 below ₹5 crore turnover and ₹7,850 above it, plus 18% GST. Service providers, EOUs and SEZ units pay different rates, and a new e-RCMC has a one-time processing fee.'],
            ['Is a FIEO RCMC valid for all products?', 'A FIEO RCMC covers multi-product exporters and products no specific council covers. If your products fall under a product-specific export promotion council, the Foreign Trade Policy expects you to take RCMC from that council.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'safety-testing-is-13252-1-2010-it-equipments',
        'title' => 'IS 13252 (Part 1) / IEC 60950-1: Meaning on Labels & Testing',
        'description' => 'What ‘IS 13252 (Part 1)/IEC 60950-1’ on a charger or device label means, how to check the R-number, testing scope, and the move to IS/IEC 62368-1.',
        'lead' => 'IS 13252 (Part 1) is the BIS safety standard for IT equipment, identical to IEC 60950-1. Products carry it with an R-number under the BIS Compulsory Registration Scheme.',
        'sections' => [
            ['What IS 13252 on a product label means', '<p>A label reading “IS 13252 (Part 1)/IEC 60950-1” next to the BIS Standard Mark and a number such as R-41234567 means the product was tested to India’s IT equipment safety standard at a BIS-recognised lab and registered with BIS under the Compulsory Registration Scheme. You see it on phone and laptop chargers, adapters, power banks, routers, printers and many other IT products. The R-number identifies the manufacturer’s registration; the model name on the same label should match the registration.</p>'],
            ['How to verify the R-number', '<ol>
<li>Note the R-number and the model name from the label.</li>
<li>Search for the R-number in the registered manufacturers search on the BIS CRS website, or use the BIS Care app.</li>
<li>Check that the registration is valid and that it covers the brand and model on the label.</li>
</ol><p>If the number is not found, or the model is not covered, the mark may be misused; report it through the BIS Care app.</p>'],
            ['IS 13252 to IS/IEC 62368-1 transition', '<p>BIS is moving IT and audio-video products from IS 13252 (Part 1) and IS 616 to <strong>IS/IEC 62368-1:2023</strong>, the hazard-based standard used worldwide. Some products already have earlier deadlines. For most IT and AV products, the old and new standards run together until <strong>1 November 2028</strong>, after which registrations must be under IS/IEC 62368-1. See <a href="/is-iec-62368-1">IS/IEC 62368-1 testing</a>, the <a href="/resources/blogs/bis-is-iec-62368-1-2023-migration-faqs-explain-simple-language">migration FAQs</a> and <a href="/certification/india/bis-crs-mark-certification">BIS CRS registration</a>.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What does IS 13252 Part 1 mean on a charger?', 'It means the charger was tested to India’s safety standard for IT equipment, identical to IEC 60950-1, and registered with BIS. The R-number printed next to it identifies the registration.'],
            ['How do I check a BIS R-number?', 'Search for the R-number in the registered manufacturers search on the BIS CRS website or in the BIS Care app. Check that the registration is valid and covers the brand and model on your product.'],
            ['Is IS 13252 replaced by IS/IEC 62368-1?', 'Yes, gradually. BIS is moving IT and audio-video products to IS/IEC 62368-1:2023; for most products, the old and new standards run together until 1 November 2028.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'certification/emea/eu-cybersecurity-compliance',
        'title' => 'EU RED Cybersecurity (EN 18031): Compliance for Wireless',
        'description' => 'EU Radio Equipment Directive cybersecurity rules (EN 18031-1/-2/-3) for connected products: scope, testing, technical file and how to prepare for CE.',
        'lead' => 'Since 1 August 2025, wireless products sold in the EU must meet the cybersecurity requirements of the Radio Equipment Directive, under Delegated Regulation (EU) 2022/30. EN 18031-1, -2 and -3 are the harmonised standards used to show compliance.',
        'sections' => [
            ['EN 18031 parts explained', '<table><thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>RED requirement</th><th>Applies to</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>EN 18031-1</td><td>Article 3(3)(d): no harm to the network</td><td>Radio equipment that can connect to the internet</td></tr>
<tr><td>EN 18031-2</td><td>Article 3(3)(e): protection of personal data and privacy</td><td>Equipment that processes personal, traffic or location data, plus toys, childcare equipment and wearables</td></tr>
<tr><td>EN 18031-3</td><td>Article 3(3)(f): protection from fraud</td><td>Internet-connected equipment that processes virtual money or monetary value</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>The standards are cited in the EU’s Official Journal <strong>with restrictions</strong>. If your product lets users skip setting a password, if parental controls are missing on toys and childcare products, or for certain secure-update criteria in EN 18031-3, the standard alone does not give a presumption of conformity, and a notified body must assess the product.</p>'],
            ['How RED cybersecurity links to CE marking', '<p>These requirements are part of the Radio Equipment Directive, so meeting them is a condition for <a href="/certification/emea/ce-certification">CE marking</a>, alongside radio, EMC and safety. The EU Declaration of Conformity must list Article 3(3)(d), (e) or (f) where they apply, together with the EN 18031 parts used. From 11 December 2027, the Cyber Resilience Act sets cybersecurity requirements for products with digital elements, and the RED delegated act is expected to give way to it.</p>'],
            ['Which products are covered by RED cybersecurity', '<ul>
<li>Phones, tablets, laptops and other internet-connected radio devices</li>
<li>Smart home devices, cameras, speakers, routers and IoT products with Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, cellular or similar radios</li>
<li>Wearables such as smartwatches and fitness trackers</li>
<li>Toys and childcare products with radio, such as baby monitors</li>
<li>Radio products that handle payments or virtual money</li>
</ul><p>Some products covered by their own EU laws, such as medical devices, vehicles and aviation equipment, are excluded from some or all of these requirements.</p>'],
            ['Testing and documentation for RED cybersecurity', '<ol>
<li>Decide which of Articles 3(3)(d), (e) and (f) apply, and which EN 18031 parts.</li>
<li>Carry out a risk assessment and document the security mechanisms: authentication, secure updates, secure storage and communication, logging, and the handling of personal data.</li>
<li>Test and assess the product against the applicable EN 18031 requirements, at an accredited lab or in-house.</li>
<li>Where a restriction applies, or the standards are not fully applied, use a notified body (EU-type examination).</li>
<li>Add the evidence to the technical file and update the EU Declaration of Conformity.</li>
</ol>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is EN 18031?', 'EN 18031 is the series of harmonised European standards for the cybersecurity requirements of the Radio Equipment Directive. Part 1 covers network protection, Part 2 personal data and privacy, and Part 3 fraud protection for products that handle money.'],
            ['Which products need RED cybersecurity compliance?', 'Radio equipment that connects to the internet, processes personal data, or handles money, as well as radio toys, childcare products and wearables, placed on the EU market from 1 August 2025. Products covered by certain other EU laws, such as medical devices and vehicles, are excluded.'],
        ],
    ],
];
